WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.040
- Giant multinational corporations

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.950
don't generally have
the best of reputations.

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:06.529
And I want to stress that
this is not an advert for McDonald's.

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:08.330
I approached them
about filming this.

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:10.770
Because almost everyone
who watches this video

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.170
will have eaten at McDonald's
at some point in their lives.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.450
I'm sure the few of you who've always
turned down the opportunity

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:17.470
will be very vocal about
it in the comments(!)

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:21.340
But for the majority of people who have
eaten at the Golden Arches, well,

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.980
the experience will be roughly
the same wherever you are.

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:25.210
That's the point of fast food chains.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:26.590
Everything's standardised.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:28.380
You get the same experience,
the same food,

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.850
no matter where you are, right?

00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:34.900
Well, when I heard that the world's
only "float-through McDonald's"

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.211
was on little canal on
the Elbe River in Germany,

00:00:38.211 --> 00:00:40.090
I thought that was probably worth a visit.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:41.910
Now, just to manage your expectations,

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:43.860
this is not a McDonald's
with a moat around it

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.370
where there's a queue of kayaks

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:47.340
lining up for each window in turn.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:48.240
That would be better.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.210
But while there was an actual
microphone and speaker here once,

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.280
a few years ago, these days
the 21st century has arrived.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.240
You have to order on the smartphone app.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.950
- We've had the McBoat since 2015.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:07.220
In the winter, we may close, yes.
But in summer and in spring, we have 24/7.

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:09.290
People are so happy to see this

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.140
because they read this
or saw this on Instagram

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.220
and they come especially for this.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:19.320
We can see your order on the screen
and we make your food ready.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.370
And one person goes on,
and brings the bag out

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:26.500
and wish them a good, "Enjoy your meal"
and a happy "good day".

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.020
- One of the things I've learned over the
years I've been making videos

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.203
is that people like a combination
of familiarity and novelty.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.490
If everything changes,
people don't like it

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:36.740
and they move to other things.

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:39.320
But if nothing changes, people get bored

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.550
and they move to other things.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:42.860
And in the same way, I think
it's fascinating to find something

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.400
that's very close to what you already know

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.950
but that changes it in one
or two interesting ways.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.170
When something like McDonald's

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:54.523
has a reputation for being
controlled and standardised

00:01:54.523 --> 00:01:56.800
and you know exactly what to expect,

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.910
well, seeing small reinventions
of that formula can be fascinating.

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:02.550
Plus, it gives them some publicity.

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:03.950
Oh, and one more thing.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:07.660
I didn't mention exactly
where in Germany we are.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:08.980
Because it is quite apt

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:11.920
that the world's only
float-through McDonald's...

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:12.910
...is in Hamburg.

